WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.512
APIs are designed for automation, which
means they can be abused at machine speed.

2
00:00:07.592 --> 00:00:13.994
Rate limiting, throttling, quotas,
resource limits, pagination limits,

3
00:00:14.074 --> 00:00:21.391
file upload limits, and expensive-query
controls help keep normal traffic reliable

4
00:00:21.471 --> 00:00:24.621
while reducing the impact of misuse.

5
00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:28.461
These controls are not only for public
APIs.

6
00:00:28.541 --> 00:00:34.232
Internal and partner APIs can also be
overloaded by bugs, retries,

7
00:00:34.312 --> 00:00:39.596
compromised credentials, or poorly
designed integrations.

8
00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:42.115
Good limits are contextual.

9
00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:47.886
A user-facing endpoint may need limits by
user, token, IP address,

10
00:00:47.966 --> 00:00:52.031
tenant, operation, and authentication
state.

11
00:00:52.111 --> 00:00:58.818
A service-to-service endpoint may need
limits by workload identity or client

12
00:00:58.898 --> 00:01:00.117
application.

13
00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:06.295
A file upload API may need size, count,
content, and processing limits.

14
00:01:06.375 --> 00:01:10.541
A search API may need query complexity
controls.

15
00:01:10.621 --> 00:01:17.430
The most effective controls reflect what
the endpoint actually costs and who is

16
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.034
allowed to use it.

17
00:01:19.114 --> 00:01:26.024
Replay risk should be considered at a high
level for operations where repeating a

18
00:01:26.104 --> 00:01:28.137
request can cause harm.

19
00:01:28.217 --> 00:01:33.399
Payments, approvals, password resets,
inventory changes,

20
00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:40.593
and webhook processing should be designed
to handle duplicate or delayed requests

21
00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:41.384
safely.

22
00:01:41.464 --> 00:01:47.968
Idempotency keys, nonce-like values,
timestamps, signature validation,

23
00:01:48.048 --> 00:01:54.552
and server-side state checks may be
appropriate depending on the workflow.

24
00:01:54.632 --> 00:02:01.136
The point is to make repeated requests
predictable rather than surprising.

25
00:02:01.216 --> 00:02:05.789
Bot and automation pressure is not always
malicious.

26
00:02:05.869 --> 00:02:10.035
A client library bug, an integration retry
loop,

27
00:02:10.115 --> 00:02:16.314
or a misconfigured job can create the same
operational pain as an attack.

28
00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:23.609
API teams should design controls that
protect availability while producing useful

29
00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:24.502
signals.

30
00:02:24.582 --> 00:02:26.208
When a limit is hit,

31
00:02:26.288 --> 00:02:32.385
the response should be clear enough for
legitimate clients to back off,

32
00:02:32.465 --> 00:02:37.953
and monitoring should show which identity,
tenant, operation,

33
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:40.980
or endpoint is creating pressure.
